<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day25" xml:id="day-25-lecture">
    <title>Day 25</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            9.2: Support Vector Classifier
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the difference between the Support Vector Classifier
                    and the Maximal Margin Classifier?
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                What shortcomings of the Maximal Margin
                                Classifier does the Support Vector Classifier
                                overcome?
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                How?
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                You should be able to describe the difference
                                verbally, graphically, and mathematically.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do you interpret each element of the mathematical
                    formulation of the Support Vector Machine graphically?
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Given a graph of a Support Vector Classifier and
                                some data points, you should be able to derive
                                the values of different parameters in the
                                mathematical formulation (e.g., <m>M</m>,
                                <m>\epsilon_i</m>).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What parameter controls the flexibility (and hence the
                    bias-variance trade-off) of the Support Vector Classifier?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What are the support vectors of a Support Vector Machine?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Support Vector Classifier (SVC): Basic idea</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Be okay with having a classifier that isn't quite
                        perfect.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Aim for greater robustness to individual observations.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Better classification of most of the training
                        observations.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The result is a support vector classifier.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Also called a <term>soft margin classifier</term>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Soft margin</title>

        <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
            <stack>
                <image source="figures/Fig9-6a.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Two side-by-side scatter plots of ten enumerated data points along with a line representing the support vector classifier, and two dashed lines that mark the threshold. The left plot shows some observations within the margin; the right one shows some misclassified training observations on the wrong side of the decision boundary.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Some points on the wrong side of the margin.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <stack>
                <image source="figures/Fig9-6b.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Some observations lie on the wrong side of the
                        hyperplane and are misclassified.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Some points on the wrong side of the hyperplane
                                (misclassified).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Mathematical formulation of SVC</title>

    <p>
  <md>
    <mrow>\amp \underset{\beta_0, \beta_1, \dots, \beta_p, \epsilon_1, \dots, \epsilon_n, M}{\text{maximize}} \quad M</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp \text{subject to} \quad \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j^2 = 1,</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp y_i(\beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{i1} + \beta_2 x_{i2} + \cdots + \beta_p x_{ip}) \geq M(1 - \epsilon_i),</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp \epsilon_i \geq 0, \quad \sum_{i=1}^{n} \epsilon_i \leq C,</mrow>
  </md>
</p>

<p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p><m>C</m> is a nonnegative tuning parameter</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><m>M</m> is the width of the margin</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><m>\epsilon_1, \dots, \epsilon_n</m> are <term>slack variables</term>:
      <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>0 \lt \epsilon_i \gt 1</m>: Allow the ith observation to be within the margin <m>M</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>\epsilon_i \gt 1</m>: Allow misclassification.
            </p>
        </li>
      </ul> </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</p>    
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
        <title>What is <m>\epsilon</m>?</title>

        <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>\epsilon_i=0</m>, then the observation is
                                on the correct side of the margin.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>\epsilon_i&gt;0</m>, then the observation
                                is on the wrong side of the margin (violated
                                margin).
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If <m>\epsilon_i&gt;1</m>, then the observation
                                is on the wrong side of the hyperplane.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>

            <image source="figures/Fig9-6b.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    Some points are on the wrong side of the hyperplane and are
                    misclassified.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

<example>
    <title>Finding the range of <m>\epsilon_i</m></title>
    <introduction>
<p>
            Fix the margin <m>M=\sqrt{2}</m>, and recall that
            <md>
                y_i(\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip})
      \geq M(1-\epsilon_i)
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            Choose the correct <m>\epsilon_i</m> range for each of the following points. 
        </p>


            <image source="figures/SVC-DesmosExample-A_and_C.png" width="60%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Support vector classifier example showing points A and C.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which of the following is true for <m>\epsilon_A</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>\epsilon_A=0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
        <p>Correct! Point A is on the correct side of the classifier, outside of the margin <m>M</m>, so <m>\epsilon_A=0</m>.</p>
        </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>0 \lt \epsilon_A \lt 0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>\epsilon_A \gt 0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        </choice>
        </choices>
    </task> 
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which of the following is true for <m>\epsilon_B</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
        <choice>
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>\epsilon_B=0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>0 \lt \epsilon_B \lt 0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
        <p>Correct! Point B is on the correct side of the classifier, but within the margin <m>M</m>, so <m>0 \lt \epsilon_B \lt 1</m>.</p>
        </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>\epsilon_B \gt 0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        </choice>
        </choices>
    </task> 
     <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which of the following is true for <m>\epsilon_C</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
        <choice>
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>\epsilon_C=0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>0 \lt \epsilon_C \lt 0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice  correct="yes">
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>\epsilon_C \gt 0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
        <p>Correct! Point C is on the wrong side of the classifier, so <m>\epsilon_C \gt 1</m>.</p>
        </feedback>
        </choice>
        </choices>
    </task> 
    
</example>

      <example>
        <title>Computing <m>\epsilon_i</m></title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                Fix <m>M=\sqrt{2}</m>, and recall that
                <md>
                    y_i(\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip})
      \geq M(1-\epsilon_i)
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Fill in the table so that the inequality is satisfied.
            </p>

            <image source="figures/SVC-DesmosExample.png" width="60%">
                <shortdescription>
                    SVC demo example showing the points used to determine the
                    slack variables.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <table>
                <title>Partially filled out table.</title>

                <tabular>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Point</cell>
                        <cell>Left Side</cell>
                        <cell><m>\epsilon_i</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>M(1-\epsilon_i)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>A</cell>
                        <cell><m>2\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>C</cell>
                        <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>X</cell>
                        <cell><m>\frac{3\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Y</cell>
                        <cell><m>\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Z</cell>
                        <cell><m>-\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </statement>

        <answer>
            <table>
                <title>Completed Table</title>

                <tabular>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Point</cell>
                        <cell>Left Side</cell>
                        <cell><m>\epsilon_i</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>M(1-\epsilon_i)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>A</cell>
                        <cell><m>2\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>B</cell>
                        <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>C</cell>
                        <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>X</cell>
                        <cell><m>\frac{3\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Y</cell>
                        <cell><m>\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Z</cell>
                        <cell><m>-\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>-\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                Total <m>\epsilon</m> is 5.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>

    

    <paragraphs>
        <title>What is <m>C</m>?</title>

        <p>
            <md>
                <mrow>
    \underset{\beta_0,\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_p,\epsilon_1,\ldots,\epsilon_n,M}{\text{maximize}}
    \quad M
  </mrow>
                <mrow>
    \text{subject to}\quad \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2=1
  </mrow>
                <mrow>
    y_i(\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip})
    \geq M(1-\epsilon_i)
  </mrow>
                <mrow>
    \epsilon_i\geq 0,\qquad \sum_{i=1}^{n}\epsilon_i\leq C
  </mrow>
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>C</m> is nonnegative tuning parameter.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Bounds the sum of the <m>\epsilon_i</m>, so it controls
                        the number and severity of margin violations (the
                        budget).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>C=0</m> means no violations are allowed.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>C &gt; 0</m> means the total amount of margin violation
                        is bounded by <m>C</m>; it does not necessarily limit
                        the number of observations on the wrong side of the
                        hyperplane to <m>C</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        In the previous example, the total of the
                        <m>\epsilon_i</m> was 5, so the hyperplane satisfies the
                        constraint for <m>C\geq 5</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <image source="figures/Fig9-6b.png" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>
                Some points are on the wrong side of the hyperplane and are
                misclassified.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Examples varying <m>C</m></title>

        <sidebyside width="25%">
            <image source="figures/Fig9-7d.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    Support vector classifier with the largest value of C among
                    the four examples.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <image source="figures/Fig9-7c.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    Support vector classifier with the second-largest value of
                    C.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <image source="figures/Fig9-7b.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    Support vector classifier with the second-smallest value of
                    C.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <image source="figures/Fig9-7a.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    Support vector classifier with the smallest value of C.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            Increasing <m>C</m> <m>\longrightarrow</m>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        As <m>C</m> increases, the margin becomes wider, so more points can violate the margin/hyperplane. 
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>C</m> controls the bias-variance trade-off:
                        <ul>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Small <m>C</m>: less flexibility, lower bias, higher
                        variance.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Large <m>C</m>: more flexibility, higher bias, lower
                        variance.
                    </p>
                </li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>What affects the hyperplane?</title>

        <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
            <image source="figures/Fig9-7d.png">
                <shortdescription>
                    Support vector classifier with a large value of C.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <stack>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Only observations on the margin or violating the
                                margin affect the hyperplane. 
                                <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                These observations are called <term>support vectors</term>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                So the support vector includes points within the margin and misclassified points.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <p>
                                            Contrast this with MMC, where the support vector is entirely composed of observations strictly on the margin. 
                                        </p>
                                    </li>
                                </ul>
                            </p>
                        </li>


                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Changing the positions of non-support vector points does not
                                affect the hyperplane.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </stack>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>TL;DR</title>


                <p>
                    <term>Maximal Margin Classifier</term>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\amp \underset{\beta_0, \beta_1, \dots, \beta_p, M}{\text{maximize}} M</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp \text{subject to } \sum_{j=1}^{p} \beta_j^2 = 1,</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp y_i(\beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{i1} + \beta_2 x_{i2} + \dots + \beta_p x_{ip}) \ge M</mrow>
                        <mrow> \quad \forall i = 1, \dots, n</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>

                <image source="figures/Fig9-3.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Maximal margin classifier separating two classes of
                        observations.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    <term>Support Vector Classifier</term>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\underset{\beta_0,\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_p,\epsilon_1,\ldots,\epsilon_n,M}{\text{maximize}} \quad M</mrow>
                        <mrow>\text{subject to}\quad \sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_j^2=1</mrow>
                        <mrow> y_i(\beta_0+\beta_1x_{i1}+\beta_2x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_px_{ip}) \geq M(1-\epsilon_i)</mrow>
                        <mrow>\epsilon_i\geq 0,\qquad \sum_{i=1}^{n}\epsilon_i\leq C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>

                <image source="figures/Fig9-7d.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Support vector classifier with a large value of C.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

    </paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Limiting factor of SVC</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <image source="figures/Fig9-8a.png">
      <shortdescription>An example showing data of two classes where one class is clustered within an ellipsoid in the center of the X1, X2 predictor space.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <image source="figures/Fig9-8b.png">
      <shortdescription>An example illustrating a limitation of the support vector classifier when used to fit data where the a hyperplane does not separate the two classes.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Requires linear decision boundaries.</p></li>
      <!-- <li><p>"Nonlinear" can mean many things. How do you choose features to learn?</p></li> -->
      <li><p>For the shown data, a linear hyperplane gives a poor fit.</p></li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Solution: Support Vector Machines (next time!)
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</paragraphs>

</handout>